  "claims": [
    {
      "quote": "A tool can run on an open model and still send its users' questions to a foreign API, ship analytics, or stop working when the connection drops.",
      "topic": "model_location"
    },
    {
      "quote": "Sigillum turns that vague promise into a measurement. It asks any AI project five questions — where does the model live, where does the data go, what happens without internet, who else is in the loop, can it be verified — finds a verifiable trace (file and line) for each answer, and summarises the result as one letter from A (fully sovereign) to E (dependent on foreign services) with five pictograms, like the energy label or the Nutri-Score.",
      "topic": "verifiability"
    },
    {
      "quote": "The grade is deterministic and rests on three published rules: the proof rule (a claim without a trace never earns more than C; missing information is shown as unknown and caps the grade at C), the weakest link (the final grade is the worst answer) and a dated, tamper-evident seal (commit, method version, SHA-256 fingerprint, 90-day expiry).",
      "topic": "verifiability"
    },
    {
      "quote": "Apertus does what fixed rules cannot: it extracts what a project claims about itself, sets each claim against the traces and names the contradictions, and writes the verdict in German, French, Italian and Romansh. Every Apertus answer is checked against the evidence before use, and rejections are counted in the report.",
      "topic": "verifiability"
    },
    {
      "quote": "Headline results: on 5 reference projects, the seal matches the expected grade in 5/5 cases, inside a container with networking disabled.",
